1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,530
An elephant walking across the savannah
comes across a pile of bones.

2
00:00:03,530 --> 00:00:04,550
She stops.

3
00:00:04,550 --> 00:00:05,970
The whole group stops.

4
00:00:05,970 --> 00:00:13,960
They gather round, and they start touching — running the tips of their
trunks over the skull, over the tusks, lifting pieces and turning them over.

5
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:17,070
Some of them put their feet
on the bones very gently.

6
00:00:17,070 --> 00:00:18,980
It goes on for several minutes.

7
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:20,420
Then they move on.

8
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:22,340
They do this for elephant bones.

9
00:00:22,340 --> 00:00:26,700
They do not do it for buffalo bones,
or rhino bones, or a piece of wood.

10
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:28,490
So she knows something.

11
00:00:28,490 --> 00:00:29,910
The question is what.

12
00:00:29,910 --> 00:00:32,410
Does she know that this was one of us?

13
00:00:32,410 --> 00:00:34,620
Does she know what happened to it?

14
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:37,640
And does she know that one
day it will happen to her?

15
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:44,410
We need to be careful here, because there are three different
questions hiding inside this one and people mix them up constantly.

16
00:00:44,410 --> 00:00:47,590
The first is: do animals react to death?

17
00:00:47,590 --> 00:00:49,300
Obviously yes.

18
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:52,060
That's not in dispute and
it isn't very interesting.

19
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:54,540
The second is: do animals grieve?

20
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:58,920
That's harder, but the behaviour is well documented
and it looks a lot like grief.

21
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:01,280
The third is the real one.

22
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:04,520
Does an animal understand
death as a concept?

23
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:05,940
That it's permanent.

24
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:07,620
That it happens to everyone.

25
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:13,900
And — the big one — that it is going to happen
to me, personally, at some point in a future I can imagine.

26
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:19,680
Developmental psychologists actually break this down, because
they study when human children get it.

27
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:21,850
There are separate components.

28
00:01:21,850 --> 00:01:23,640
That the body stops working.

29
00:01:23,640 --> 00:01:25,350
That it can't be undone.

30
00:01:25,350 --> 00:01:27,740
That it happens to every living thing.

31
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:29,790
That there are physical causes.

32
00:01:29,790 --> 00:01:31,650
And that it applies to you.

33
00:01:31,650 --> 00:01:36,420
Children pick these up in pieces,
usually somewhere between four and seven.

34
00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:39,860
The last one to arrive is
almost always the personal one.

35
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:42,370
So we're not asking
whether animals are sad.

36
00:01:42,370 --> 00:01:44,940
We're asking how much of
that list they have.

37
00:01:44,940 --> 00:01:49,030
Back to the elephants, because
it's the best-studied case.

38
00:01:49,030 --> 00:02:00,110
Researchers laid out different objects for wild elephant families to find: elephant
skulls, rhino skulls, buffalo skulls, pieces of ivory, and plain wood.

39
00:02:00,110 --> 00:02:03,910
The elephants went for the
ivory and the elephant skulls.

40
00:02:03,910 --> 00:02:11,000
They spent significantly more time investigating them, touching
and smelling them, than anything else on offer.

41
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:12,650
So they can tell.

42
00:02:12,650 --> 00:02:19,100
An elephant skull registers as a different category of
object to an elephant than a buffalo skull does.

43
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:21,550
And this isn't a one-off.

44
00:02:21,550 --> 00:02:30,780
Elephants have been recorded standing over dead members of their group for hours, trying
to lift them, covering them with earth and branches, returning to the place afterwards.

45
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:37,780
There are accounts of elephants staying with a body through the night,
and of a group refusing to leave until the smell has changed.

46
00:02:37,780 --> 00:02:43,470
Keepers in sanctuaries describe animals going off their food
for days after a death in the herd.

47
00:02:43,470 --> 00:02:46,310
Now — the honest reading.

48
00:02:46,310 --> 00:02:50,900
All of that shows recognition and something
that looks a great deal like distress.

49
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:55,160
It does not, by itself, show
an understanding that death is permanent.

50
00:02:55,160 --> 00:03:02,100
An elephant trying to lift a dead companion might be doing
so precisely because it does not understand that this is over.

51
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:04,100
It might be trying to wake her up.

52
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:09,720
Which is a strange and slightly awful thought, and it
is going to come back later in this video.

53
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:12,550
Then there's the case that
went round the world.

54
00:03:12,550 --> 00:03:16,790
In twenty eighteen, an orca off
the coast of Washington gave birth.

55
00:03:16,790 --> 00:03:19,180
The calf lived about half an hour.

56
00:03:19,180 --> 00:03:21,160
And the mother did not let it sink.

57
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:27,470
She carried the body on her head, pushing it up through the
water, and when it slipped off she dived and retrieved it.

58
00:03:27,470 --> 00:03:37,910
She did this for seventeen days, across more than a thousand kilometres, while her family group
stayed with her and — according to the observers — at times helped carry it.

59
00:03:37,910 --> 00:03:40,450
She stopped eating properly.

60
00:03:40,450 --> 00:03:41,890
She fell behind the group.

61
00:03:41,890 --> 00:03:43,800
The other whales waited.

62
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:46,500
This behaviour is not unique to her.

63
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:57,520
Primatologists have documented chimpanzee and macaque mothers carrying dead infants for days
and sometimes weeks, continuing to groom them, until the body is unrecognisable.

64
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:01,780
It is very hard to watch that
and conclude nothing is happening inside.

65
00:04:01,780 --> 00:04:04,670
But notice what it does not tell us.

66
00:04:04,670 --> 00:04:11,160
Carrying a dead infant for seventeen days is,
if anything, evidence of not understanding permanence.

67
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:16,830
It is what a mother does when the alarm that says
my baby is missing has no way to be switched off.

68
00:04:16,830 --> 00:04:18,680
The grief may be real.

69
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:21,180
That doesn't mean the concept is.

70
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:26,130
Now for a case that looks like a funeral
and turns out to be something much more practical.

71
00:04:26,130 --> 00:04:29,450
When a crow finds a
dead crow, other crows come.

72
00:04:29,450 --> 00:04:34,500
They gather in the trees around it,
they call loudly, and a crowd builds.

73
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:37,700
It has been called a crow
funeral for a hundred years.

74
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:40,310
So researchers tested it properly.

75
00:04:40,310 --> 00:04:45,940
They had a person hold a dead crow while
standing in a particular spot, wearing a distinctive mask.

76
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:48,680
The crows mobbed and alarm-called.

77
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:50,100
Fine.

78
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:52,650
But then came the interesting part.

79
00:04:52,650 --> 00:04:55,990
For weeks afterwards, those
crows avoided that place.

80
00:04:55,990 --> 00:04:58,060
And they remembered the face.

81
00:04:58,060 --> 00:05:02,210
When the same mask appeared later, without
any dead crow, they scolded it.

82
00:05:02,210 --> 00:05:04,230
So it isn't mourning.

83
00:05:04,230 --> 00:05:06,000
It's intelligence gathering.

84
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:12,080
A dead crow is information: something killed a crow
here, and possibly that thing is standing right there.

85
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:17,250
Learn the location, learn the face,
tell everybody, don't go back.

86
00:05:17,250 --> 00:05:19,930
This is arguably more
impressive than grief.

87
00:05:19,930 --> 00:05:23,510
It's an animal using another animal's
death as data about the world.

88
00:05:23,510 --> 00:05:26,830
But it tells you nothing about
whether the crow knows it's mortal.

89
00:05:26,830 --> 00:05:32,290
So what would an animal actually need, in order
to know that it is going to die?

90
00:05:32,290 --> 00:05:34,030
Not sadness.

91
00:05:34,030 --> 00:05:35,170
Not memory.

92
00:05:35,170 --> 00:05:42,160
It needs to be able to place itself in a future that does not
exist yet, and imagine a state of the world it will never experience.

93
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:51,980
That's a very specific cognitive trick, and it's the same one that lets you dread
a dentist appointment on Thursday or feel nervous about a conversation that hasn't happened.

94
00:05:51,980 --> 00:05:56,000
For a long time the assumption
was that only humans do this.

95
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:00,730
That animals live in a permanent present,
dealing with what's in front of them.

96
00:06:00,730 --> 00:06:03,250
That assumption is now in trouble.

97
00:06:03,250 --> 00:06:05,320
Scrub jays hide food.

98
00:06:05,320 --> 00:06:17,080
And in experiments, they hide food for a tomorrow they are not currently experiencing — given a situation where
they'll be hungry in a particular room in the morning, they'll cache food in that room the night before.

99
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:19,120
They aren't responding to hunger now.

100
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:21,330
They're solving a problem
they will have later.

101
00:06:21,330 --> 00:06:26,720
Great apes have been shown selecting and keeping a tool
they'll need for a task hours in the future.

102
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:30,120
So the ability to think about
the future isn't uniquely ours.

103
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:32,040
It's a matter of degree.

104
00:06:32,040 --> 00:06:35,740
And that makes the line we drew
a lot blurrier than it was.

105
00:06:35,740 --> 00:06:39,770
Here it is, and it is going
to be less satisfying than you want.

106
00:06:39,770 --> 00:06:41,240
We don't know.

107
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:49,030
And there's a decent argument that we can't know, because the only way to be
sure would be to ask, and the answer would have to come back in language.

108
00:06:49,030 --> 00:06:51,550
What we can say is roughly this.

109
00:06:51,550 --> 00:06:56,320
Plenty of animals clearly understand that something has
changed and that the change is bad.

110
00:06:56,320 --> 00:06:59,520
Several show behaviour
indistinguishable from grief.

111
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:02,630
Some use death as
information about danger.

112
00:07:02,630 --> 00:07:06,010
A few can plan for futures
they are not currently in.

113
00:07:06,010 --> 00:07:21,000
What no animal has ever demonstrated is the specific, awful package that humans carry around: the knowledge that their own death is
certain, that it applies to them personally, that it could be at any time, and that nothing can be done about it.

114
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:28,840
And there is a real possibility — this is the part I
find genuinely unsettling — that some animals have parts of it.

115
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:30,710
Not the full picture.

116
00:07:30,710 --> 00:07:35,470
Just enough to know that something is very wrong,
without the framework to make sense of it.

117
00:07:35,470 --> 00:07:39,970
An elephant standing over bones for twenty
minutes might be somewhere in that gap.

118
00:07:39,970 --> 00:07:43,740
Which turns the question around,
and points it at you.

119
00:07:43,740 --> 00:07:46,600
Because we're the ones
who definitely have it.

120
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:48,300
And it is not free.

121
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:52,310
The knowledge of your own death is running
in the background of everything you do.

122
00:07:52,310 --> 00:07:56,130
It's in the will, in the insurance, in
the health check you've been putting off.

123
00:07:56,130 --> 00:08:03,510
It's in every religion humans have ever built — practically all of
them are, among other things, an answer to this exact problem.

124
00:08:03,510 --> 00:08:08,760
It's in the pyramids, in the
gravestones, in wanting to be remembered.

125
00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:15,190
Whole cultures exist as a response to a piece of
information that no other animal appears to be burdened with.

126
00:08:15,190 --> 00:08:20,590
An elephant standing over the bones of her mother
is, as far as we can tell, sad.

127
00:08:20,590 --> 00:08:22,260
You would also be sad.

128
00:08:22,260 --> 00:08:30,760
And on top of that you would be doing something she almost certainly isn't:
looking at those bones and understanding, precisely, that you're looking at a preview.

129
00:08:30,760 --> 00:08:33,990
So — do animals know they're going to die?

130
00:08:33,990 --> 00:08:38,770
The best answer available is: probably
not the way you do.

131
00:08:38,770 --> 00:08:40,460
They have the feelings.

132
00:08:40,460 --> 00:08:43,200
They mostly seem to lack the sentence.

133
00:08:43,200 --> 00:08:44,910
And that's the trade.

134
00:08:44,910 --> 00:08:47,280
They get the grief without the dread.

135
00:08:47,280 --> 00:08:56,370
We got the dread, and in exchange we got planning, and medicine, and
history, and every attempt anybody has ever made to leave something behind.

136
00:08:56,370 --> 00:09:02,510
The elephant touches the bones, and then walks away,
and does not spend the afternoon thinking about herself.

137
00:09:02,510 --> 00:09:05,290
There's a version of that
which sounds like a loss.

138
00:09:05,290 --> 00:09:07,510
And a version that sounds like mercy.

139
00:09:07,510 --> 00:09:13,800
And if you're now wondering what else you have that no
other animal has to carry...
...that's a question for another video.
